{
  "summary": "Implements the BIP 54 (Consensus Cleanup) validation rules in Bitcoin Core, defining a BIP 9 versionbits deployment on bit 3 named consensuscleanup. The rules are configured to be always active on regtest and never active on mainnet, testnet, or signet. The implementation enforces a 2,500 non-witness sigops limit per transaction, invalidates 64-byte transactions, requires coinbase transactions to be timelocked to block height minus one with non-final sequence numbers, and constrains block timestamps to prevent timewarp attacks and negative difficulty adjustment intervals.",
  "problem": "Bitcoin consensus retains known vulnerabilities and algorithmic weaknesses: quadratic legacy sigops CPU exhaustion, Merkle tree leaf-versus-internal node collisions for 64-byte transactions (CVE-2017-12842), and timewarp or negative difficulty adjustments that can manipulate proof-of-work retargeting. Bitcoin Core currently has no implementation or comprehensive regression test harness for BIP 54, making it impossible to evaluate or prepare the consensus cleanup soft fork within the upstream repository.",
  "evidence": [
    "The underlying validation rules address documented consensus flaws, including CVE-2017-12842 (Merkle tree ambiguity) and CPU exhaustion from unconstrained legacy script sigops.",
    "PR description notes the code was previously implemented, reviewed, and tested on Bitcoin Inquisition (PRs #99 and #110, plus Delving Bitcoin demonstrations of slow blocks on signet) before being brought upstream with improved tests and test vectors."
  ],
  "dependencies": {
